LESSON 3: FETCHING DATA FROM THE BACKEND THE RIGHT WAY
WITH THE useEffect HOOK
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on fetching backend data safely from a React component.

You will understand:

• Fetching data after rendering

• Using useEffect for external synchronization

• Keeping network requests out of render

• Managing data state

• Managing loading state

• Managing error state

• Checking HTTP response status

• Using dependency arrays correctly

• Cancelling obsolete requests

• Avoiding stale-response race conditions

• Rendering loading/error/success UI

• Understanding development Strict Mode behavior


================================================================================
WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
================================================================================

In Lesson 2 we saw several incorrect patterns:

Render
 ↓
fetch()
 ↓
setState()
 ↓
Render
 ↓
fetch()


A better architecture is:

Render
 ↓
React Commits UI
 ↓
useEffect
 ↓
HTTP Request
 ↓
Backend
 ↓
Response
 ↓
setState()
 ↓
Render Updated UI


CORE RULE:

RENDERING
should calculate the UI.

useEffect
should synchronize with external systems when an Effect is the appropriate
owner of that synchronization.


================================================================================
PART 2: WHAT PROBLEM ARE WE SOLVING?
================================================================================

WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

function MyComponent() {

  fetch(
    '/api/data'
  )

  return (...)
}


Problem:

The network request starts during render.


================================================================================
BETTER
================================================================================

function MyComponent() {

  useEffect(() => {

    // Start synchronization here.

  }, [])


  return (...)
}


Now:

Render
 ↓
Commit
 ↓
Effect
 ↓
Request


================================================================================
COMMON PROBLEMS
================================================================================

+-------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| PROBLEM                 | RESULT                                     |
+-------------------------+--------------------------------------------+
| fetch() during render   | Impure rendering/repeated requests         |
| Wrong dependencies      | Missing or excessive fetching              |
| No HTTP check           | 404/500 may be handled incorrectly         |
| No error state          | User receives no useful failure feedback   |
| No loading state        | User doesn't know request is pending       |
| Request race            | Older response may overwrite newer data    |
| Obsolete request        | Unnecessary external work continues        |
+-------------------------+--------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
PART 3: BASIC useEffect FETCH PATTERN
================================================================================

IMPORT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import {

  useEffect,

  useState

} from 'react'


================================================================================
BASIC STRUCTURE
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const loadData =
    async () => {

      // Fetch backend data.

    }


  loadData()

}, [])


================================================================================
WHY IS THE ASYNC FUNCTION INSIDE THE EFFECT?
================================================================================

Avoid:

useEffect(
  async () => {

    const response =
      await fetch(...)

  },
  []
)


An async function returns:

Promise


But an Effect callback should return:

nothing

or:

a cleanup function


Therefore use:

useEffect(() => {

  const loadData =
    async () => {

      ...
    }


  loadData()


  return () => {

    // Optional cleanup.

  }

}, [])


