Therefore our previous architecture still works:

App
│
├── groceryItems
├── handleAddItem
├── handleToggleBought
├── handleRemoveItem
│
├── AddGroceryForm
│
└── GroceryList


Only the initial data source changes:

BEFORE:

FAKE_GROCERIES


AFTER:

BACKEND API


================================================================================
PART 10: WHAT useEffect FIXES AND WHAT IT DOESN'T
================================================================================

useEffect HELPS WITH:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Keeping fetch outside rendering

• Defining synchronization dependencies

• Cleanup

• Request cancellation

• Responding to resource identifiers


================================================================================
useEffect DOES NOT AUTOMATICALLY SOLVE:
================================================================================

• HTTP error handling

• Loading UI

• Request cancellation

• Data validation

• Authentication

• Retries

• Caching

• Pagination

• Deduplication

• Race conditions unless handled correctly

• Server-side failures


You still need to implement appropriate request behavior.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT: DON'T SAY "ALWAYS USE useEffect FOR DATA FETCHING"
================================================================================

That is too broad.


Sometimes data fetching is handled by:

• Framework loaders

• Server Components

• Router data APIs

• Dedicated server-state libraries

• Event handlers

• Application-specific data layers


Within the client-side Effect-based pattern taught here:

useEffect


is appropriate for synchronizing a component with a backend resource.


================================================================================
EVENT-DRIVEN REQUESTS
================================================================================

Suppose:

User clicks:

Save Grocery


The POST request may naturally belong in:

handleSubmit()


Example conceptual flow:

Submit Button
     ↓
handleSubmit()
     ↓
POST /api/groceries


You do NOT need:

setShouldSave(true)
     ↓
useEffect observes shouldSave
     ↓
POST


just to make the request happen.


